<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Insular Government of the Philippine Islands</h1><p class="st-meta">1901 CE – 1935 CE · capital Manila</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">American civilian colonial government of the Philippines from 1901, which introduced mass schooling and elected Filipino legislatures.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In July 1901 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-commission">Philippine Commission</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-howard-taft">William Howard Taft</a> took over civil government from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/united-states-military-government-of-the-philippine-islands">United States Military Government of the Philippine Islands</a>, with Taft as the first civil governor. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-organic-act-1902">Philippine Organic Act</a> of 1902 set its framework, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bureau-of-insular-affairs">Bureau of Insular Affairs</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The governor-general and commission ruled alongside an elected Philippine Assembly from 1907. American teachers, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomasites">Thomasites</a>, spread English-language public schooling, and roads, health services and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> exports to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> grew. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jones-law-philippines">Jones Act</a> of 1916 created an all-Filipino legislature and promised independence, and Governor-General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-burton-harrison">Francis Burton Harrison</a> filled the civil service with Filipinos.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Campaigns by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-l-quezon">Manuel Quezon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sergio-osmena">Sergio Osmena</a> led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tydings-mcduffie-act">Tydings-McDuffie Act</a> of 1934, and in November 1935 the Insular Government was replaced by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commonwealth-of-the-philippines">Commonwealth of the Philippines</a> as a transition to independence.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/insular-government-of-the-philippine-islands.jpg" alt="Insular Government of the Philippine Islands" width="320" height="136"><figcaption>Image: User 50 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Philippines_(1919%E2%80%931936).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1901 CE – 1935 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Manila</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>William Howard Taft, Francis Burton Harrison, Leonard Wood</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Spanish, Tagalog</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/philippines">Philippines</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Philippine Islands, US Philippine Islands</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1918&amp;focus=insular-government-of-the-philippine-islands" aria-label="Insular Government of the Philippine Islands on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7989 1634 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Insular Government of the Philippine Islands lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8309 1890L8392 2060M8309 1890L8309 1890M8309 1890L2570 1008M8309 1890L8309 1890M8309 1890L8379 2176"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8392 2060h0M8309 1890h0M2570 1008h0M8309 1890h0M8379 2176h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8309 1890h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8309 1890h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Manila. <a href="/atlas?year=1918&amp;focus=insular-government-of-the-philippine-islands">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
